WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:07.520
Welcome back to Curious Archive. This video is&nbsp;
the second part of a two-part series exploring the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:14.240
incredible speculative biology project of Snaiad:&nbsp;
a planet teeming with incredible alien life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:19.040
Snaiad was created by C.M. Kosemen — an&nbsp;
expectational artist and the creator of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:24.160
all tomorrows. As usual, I have links below&nbsp;
where you can follow and support his work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:30.160
When we left off, we’d visited many of the basic&nbsp;
lifeforms on Snaiad. Now, it’s time to study&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:36.560
aliens of greater sizes and stranger biology — in&nbsp;
a journey that will reveal secrets from Snaiad’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:42.960
mysterious past, and perhaps its unexpected future&nbsp;
with humans….and even some never-before-seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:50.720
images from Kosemen’s upcoming book. So let’s&nbsp;
get started. On the curious island of Mapag,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:56.880
something on the beaches is walking upright&nbsp;
on two legs. This is an Armless Blumbomen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:02.240
a creature whose facial pouch — in which it is&nbsp;
currently carrying its baby — is so pronounced, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:08.560
almost looks humanoid. Are the Armless Blumbomen&nbsp;
Snaiad’s approximation of intelligent life?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:14.160
Not quite. These bipedal oddities are actually&nbsp;
closer to earth penguins than they are to us:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:19.840
as they’re cumbersome on land but well-adapted for&nbsp;
the water. But the intelligence of these creatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:25.360
isn’t distinct from other creatures on Snaiad.&nbsp;
Indeed, the author imagines no species currently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:30.400
living on the planet’s surface possess advanced&nbsp;
intellect… But looking into the archeological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:35.600
record of Snaiad, there’s evidence that&nbsp;
intelligence may have existed in the distant past.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:41.120
According to Kosemen, 30-million-year-old stone&nbsp;
axe-heads have been discovered, but there are no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:47.280
clues as to their origins. Metallic artifacts of&nbsp;
an apparently artificial nature called the ‘Dosadi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:53.040
Screws’ have been dated to even early. As Snaiad&nbsp;
is almost twice as old as Earth, it seems that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:59.520
perhaps civilization has risen multiple times only&nbsp;
to be obliterated and erased by mass extinction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:05.920
But all this is uncertain… The creatures that&nbsp;
currently wander across Snaiad, however, are truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:11.680
fascinating regardless of their intelligence. On&nbsp;
numerous offshore islands, the beaches are buzzing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:17.360
with the sounds of Grey Blumbomen — a related&nbsp;
type of Blumbomeniform. Like their larger cousins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:23.040
these somewhat awkward looking creatures are also&nbsp;
most graceful under the waves — able to chase down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:29.440
numerous marine organisms almost like tiny seals.&nbsp;
Hilariously, Kosemen mentions the Grey Blumbomen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:34.720
will one day become a major symbol in the popular&nbsp;
culture of human settlers, and will even form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:40.400
the basis for cartoon characters and children’s&nbsp;
toys. That’s right: humans will eventually create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:45.840
settlements on Snaiad. Kosemen states that in&nbsp;
the future of this narrative, human history will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:51.200
become intertwined with that of Snaiad’s as we&nbsp;
create the first settlements on this alien world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:56.960
The idea of humans trying to coexist with&nbsp;
these awe-inspiring yet often terrifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:03.200
creatures is certainly an interesting one. And one&nbsp;
important future animal to human settlers is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:09.120
Orca Runners: mighty herbivores with distinctive&nbsp;
coloring and a peaceful demeanor. Thanks to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:14.320
fast-running speeds and friendly temperament,&nbsp;
these unusual creatures will, surprisingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:20.160
become critical domesticated animals similar&nbsp;
to horses in the lives of the first settlers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:25.040
This is a telltale sign that humans and the&nbsp;
planet’s fantastical native life will have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:33.600
wide range of surprising interactions. The windy&nbsp;
crags of the Pontic mountains conceal one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:40.240
Snaiad’s most spectacular animals. The aptly named&nbsp;
‘Spinies’ possess foreclaws, first-head beaks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:46.400
and dorsal spines all elongated into javelin-like&nbsp;
spikes. Despite appearances, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:52.080
the Spinies are herbivorous and have evolved&nbsp;
these spikes for defense… so they’re actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:58.400
closer to a hedgehog than they are to some apex&nbsp;
super-predator. Kosemen imagines human colonists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:03.280
rarely glimpse spinies due to their shyness,&nbsp;
instead only hearing the haunting sounds they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:08.400
produce by rattling their spikes against&nbsp;
each other… Across the mainland continent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:15.600
a hulking, armored lifeform is on the move. This&nbsp;
is the Chasmotaurus — a type of Allotauriform:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:21.680
an extremely successful lineage of large Snaiadi&nbsp;
herbivores. Unlike its more amiable cousins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:27.200
however, Chasmotaurus prefers to be left alone:&nbsp;
and will charge down and trample anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:33.040
that invades its territory much like an Earth&nbsp;
rhinoceros. And since they grow up to 22 feet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:39.600
or 7 meters long, their poor attitude will make&nbsp;
them difficult for colonists to study… In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:46.240
misty forests of midland, a much smaller organism&nbsp;
wanders among the trees. The Austrotaurus is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:52.480
Allotauriform which has shrunk considerably due to&nbsp;
its forest habitat, and rarely exceeds six feet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:59.040
or two meters in length. Far from defenseless, the&nbsp;
Austrotaurus has evolved armadillo-like bands of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:04.880
segmented body armor, which keeps the tiny titans&nbsp;
safe from predators like the Fututoriformes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:11.680
In all manner of regions across Snaiad, you&nbsp;
might find the fututoriformes on the prowl.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:16.960
This particular species is an Arbophobos&nbsp;
— a species whose name alludes to its tree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:23.360
climbing abilities, but is indeed able to conquer&nbsp;
most any environment. Like most fututoriformes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:29.520
Arbophobus is an agile, adaptive hunter similar&nbsp;
in many respects to the large cat species of our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:34.400
own planet. Indeed, the deadliness of this&nbsp;
predator would end up being its undoing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:39.760
as these fearless hunters would attack countless&nbsp;
settlers in the early days of human exploration.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:44.560
As a result, the Arbophobus would&nbsp;
be purposely hunted to extinction…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:50.720
But there’s another hunter early settlers&nbsp;
will fear even more than the Arbophobus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:56.160
A creature they’ll know through its shrill screams&nbsp;
on dark nights, and the glints of huge eyes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:01.440
That is, until cameras eventually reveal these&nbsp;
nocturnal organisms are barely over three feet, or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:07.760
one meter long. Called infernocyons, these timid&nbsp;
hunters don’t deserve their frightening name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:13.120
as they only use their scissor-like jaws&nbsp;
to feed on small animals, and simply have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:19.840
unusually loud calls and large eyes for their&nbsp;
body-size. In the nearby peaks of Oroland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:25.840
a relative of the infernocyons lives in seclusion.&nbsp;
These are the Selenocyon, the largest species of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:32.320
their order, and critically endangered. Kosemen&nbsp;
notes they often hunt near forest streams at dusk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:38.000
with the starry sky reflecting off their&nbsp;
enormous, haunting eyes. It will be a truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:43.600
sad loss if these fascinating animals fade&nbsp;
into extinction sometime in Snaiad’s future.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:51.600
One species with a far more certain future can be&nbsp;
found in abundance north of the Divider mountains.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:57.440
This is a Pescidon, a bipedal omnivore&nbsp;
with a curious penchant for cooperation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:02.080
One of the most social animals on Snaiad,&nbsp;
Pescidons are known to coordinate hunts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:07.920
in large family groups, with similar dynamics to&nbsp;
wolf packs on Earth. Their affinity towards social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:13.360
bonding will make them a favorite domesticated&nbsp;
animal among future human settlers — with their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:19.440
path mirroring that of humans and dogs… if dogs&nbsp;
were ten feet, or three meters long that is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:25.760
Upon the elevated plateaus of the south-east,&nbsp;
another animal that will have a key role in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:31.440
humanity’s future is waiting. These bizarre,&nbsp;
spherical creatures — whose first-head faces have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:37.040
become mashed directed into their bodies — are&nbsp;
actually cousins of the Pescidons we just visited.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:41.760
And indeed, these hulking, heavy beasts&nbsp;
are of a similar size… which will make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:47.120
them ideal draft animals filling a role similar&nbsp;
to earth cows in the lives of early settlers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:54.000
Speaking of early settlers, during the first&nbsp;
aerial surveys of Snaiad, vast paths across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:59.520
the landscape will be glimpsed, and at first&nbsp;
assumed to be the creation of intelligent species.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:04.720
In reality, however, these lines are the&nbsp;
trails of the unintelligent Tractorsnakes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:09.440
massive herbivores of the ‘lophophid’&nbsp;
classification who vacuum the land for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:16.720
vegetation like living combine harvesters. At&nbsp;
an astonishing 22 feet, or 7 meters long, these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:21.520
giants might not be great thinkers, but they’re&nbsp;
truly impressive shapers of their environment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:27.120
At the edge of the Ankyranian Desert, a more&nbsp;
modest sized Lophophid slithers across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:33.760
sands. This is a Phytophyton — a lifeform that&nbsp;
at first glance shows a high degree of convergent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:39.200
evolution with snakes here on earth. Unlike all&nbsp;
types of snakes we’re familiar with, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:44.560
the Phytophyton isn’t a hunter, but instead&nbsp;
a generalist herbivore like their larger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:49.120
Tractorsnake cousin. A fascinating lifeform. If&nbsp;
you’re looking for a carnivorous snake analogue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:56.720
the sprogland along the eastern plains contains&nbsp;
the closest thing. Say hello to a Chasmostomos:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.720 --> 00:09:01.120
a rather odd-looking fellow that is a&nbsp;
representative of the poorly-understood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:07.680
Sprogophidian classficiation. While a predator,&nbsp;
at only 7 inches, or 20 centimeters long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:13.760
they’re not the most frighting thing on Snaiad.&nbsp;
Yet they do have one truly fascinating adaptation:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:18.720
the ability to shed their fatty tails when&nbsp;
attacked — in a strategy not dissimilar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:24.160
to some types of geckos. Unlike geckos&nbsp;
however, Chasmostomos tails don’t grow back.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:31.040
We’ve now seen species from all over Snaiad,&nbsp;
and have learned about the future role they’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:36.640
play in the lives of human settlers. But there’s&nbsp;
still one great frontier that we’ve yet to chart:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:44.000
the massive oceans. Taking the common Jetshark&nbsp;
as our baseline for underwater life, we’ll see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:50.480
that life in the oceans is vastly different from&nbsp;
on land. The group the Jetshark belongs to, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:57.280
jetocetes, take the two-headed Snaiadi bodyplan in&nbsp;
a highly unexpected direction. Their first heads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.280 --> 00:10:03.120
are connected to their digestive tract: and their&nbsp;
second heads have become an almost jet-like pump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:08.240
connected to an entirely different system.&nbsp;
That’s right: much of undersea Snaiadi life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:14.400
is jet-powered, kind of like some species of&nbsp;
squids on earth, just taken to a new extreme.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:20.080
The jetshark has found, unsurprisingly, a niche&nbsp;
similar to a shark in the underwater foodchain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:27.680
Far to the north, in the frigid polar waters, you&nbsp;
can find a very different kind of undersea giant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:34.880
Meet the Hadrocetoides: a 22 foot, or 7-meter-long&nbsp;
specimen that is similarly jet-powered. Protected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:40.560
from the cold with layers of thick blubber, they&nbsp;
travel in large family pods much like the mighty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:46.800
orcas that swim through our own oceans. Unlike&nbsp;
the predacious orcas, however, the Hadrocetoides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:53.280
are peaceful filter feeders… And elsewhere in&nbsp;
the ocean, the fantastic conclusion to our tour&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:59.920
of life on Snaiad is waiting. The Xenoposeidon&nbsp;
Thalassarcos is a truly awe-inspiring beast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:06.320
and the largest animal on the planet at a&nbsp;
staggering 130 feet or, 40 meters in length.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:12.080
Their cavernous beaks have an ingenious system of&nbsp;
inlets and holes that allow them to filter huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:18.080
amounts of plankton from the water — giving them&nbsp;
the food they need to reach such incredible sizes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:23.200
Of course, these creatures are most similar to&nbsp;
Earth whales in their size and feeding behavior.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:28.880
But what relationship will future humans have with&nbsp;
this animal? That’s a detail the author has yet to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.880 --> 00:11:35.520
reveal… And many more details regarding Snaiad&nbsp;
might be revealed later, as Kosemen is working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:41.360
on a meticulous remake of the project: which thus&nbsp;
far has yielded some incredible re-imaginings of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:46.800
familiar Snaiadi organisms. He gave me a few&nbsp;
never-before-seen images from the upcoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:51.760
book to share which I’m showing with you now,&nbsp;
and in my opinion, these new designs are even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:57.680
better than the old ones. If you’re a fan of this&nbsp;
project and like me want to see a book someday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.680 --> 00:12:02.400
please follow and support Kosemen in the&nbsp;
links in the description below! And as always,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:07.440
thanks for watching. If you enjoyed this entry,&nbsp;
please lend your support and like, subscribe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:23.840
and hit the notification icon to stay up to date&nbsp;
on all things Curious. See you in the next video.

